# Install

Download the file for your system from the [download section](https://highercomve.github.io/GhostPen/#download)
or the [GitHub releases](https://github.com/highercomve/GhostPen/releases).

- **Linux**: the `.deb` (Debian, Ubuntu) and `.rpm` (Fedora, openSUSE)
  install GhostPen with what it needs (GTK 4, WebKitGTK 6.0). The AppImage
  runs without installing, on a system that has those libraries. They run
  captions, dictation and Built-in models on the GPU through Vulkan (any
  GPU vendor). With an NVIDIA GPU (RTX 20xx or newer) and the CUDA 13
  runtime installed (Arch: `cuda`; others: NVIDIA's CUDA packages), the
  **NVIDIA CUDA** downloads are 10–20% faster; without the runtime they
  use Vulkan too, and they update to CUDA builds.
- **Windows**: run the installer; it installs for your user, no
  administrator needed. Captions,
  dictation and Built-in models run on any GPU through Vulkan (the CPU
  without a Vulkan driver). Windows may warn about an unknown publisher until
  the installer is code-signed: choose *More info → Run anyway*.
- **macOS**: open the `.dmg` and drag GhostPen to Applications. The app
  isn't notarized by Apple yet, so the first start is blocked ("Apple could
  not verify GhostPen…"): choose **Done**, then **System Settings → Privacy
  & Security → Open Anyway** and confirm. (Or, in Terminal:
  `xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/GhostPen.app`.) Then macOS
  asks for **Accessibility**, which GhostPen needs to copy your selection
  and paste the result.

GhostPen starts in the tray (menu bar on macOS). Its menu has Dictation,
Captions, Meetings, Playground and Settings.

# Set up your AI

GhostPen needs a model to talk to. In **Settings → AI Profiles**, a
profile runs on one of two things:

- **Built-in**: GhostPen runs the model itself, with the llama.cpp
  compiled into it. Nothing is sent over the network and no other software
  is needed. Download a model in **Settings → Built-in models** and press
  **Use**: this creates a *Built-in (GhostPen)* profile and makes it active.
- **An AI endpoint**: any OpenAI-compatible API. Presets fill in Ollama
  (`http://localhost:11434/v1`), LM Studio, OpenAI, OpenRouter and Groq;
  **Fetch models** lists what the endpoint offers.

You can keep several profiles and switch between them; the menu shows
which one is active.

## Built-in models

| Model | Size | Good for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Qwen3.5 2B | 1.3 GB | the fastest; fine for proofreading |
| Gemma 3 4B | 2.5 GB | a good all-rounder, many languages |
| Qwen3.5 4B | 2.7 GB | balanced |
| Gemma 4 E4B | 5.0 GB | recommended |
| Qwen3.5 9B | 6.0 GB | the best quality; needs about 8 GB of GPU memory |

Downloads can be paused and resumed, and are checked against the SHA-256
that Hugging Face publishes. GGUF models already downloaded by LM Studio
are found and offered too.

The model runs on the GPU when there is one (on Linux through Vulkan, on
any GPU vendor; Metal on macOS; CUDA in a `-Dcuda` build): as many of its layers as the free GPU memory holds, the rest on
the CPU. **Runner** settings: the context window (8k tokens by default),
the GPU on or off, and how long the model stays loaded after its last use
(10 minutes; it loads again in a few seconds).

# Use it

## In any app

1. Select text.
2. Press `Ctrl+Shift+A` (or click the tray icon).
3. Pick an action with the mouse or its number key.

The result replaces your selection, and your clipboard is restored
afterwards. **Intensity** (Subtle, Balanced, Strong) sets how far a rewrite
goes. The prompt bar at the bottom takes any instruction ("make it a
bulleted list", "answer politely"). Press `Esc` to close the menu,
or to stop an answer the built-in model is still writing.

Your own actions (**Settings → Custom Actions**) appear in the menu and
the Playground next to the built-in ones.

## Playground

A window to try actions on text you paste, with the answer streaming in.
Open it from the tray or the flask icon in the menu.

```=html
<a id="link-summaries"></a>
```

## Link summaries

Select a webpage link, open GhostPen, and choose **Summarize link**, or paste
the URL into the Summary window. Pick **Brief**, **Standard**, or **Detailed**
to choose how much context to keep. The answer streams into a reader with
adjustable text size, serif or sans-serif type, and comfortable or compact
spacing.

**Read summary** reads the answer aloud with the built-in voice, and
**Read page** reads the source page. The **Show thinking** switch requests
thinking on the next summary and reveals any the model returns. Some models
do not expose it. Copy, Read summary, and exports use the answer itself.

### Export a summary

1. Open **Export** in the reader toolbar.
2. Choose **PDF** for a formatted document, or **Markdown (.md)** for editable
   text with the original Mermaid diagram source.
3. Choose where to save the file in the save dialog.

Mermaid diagrams render in the reader and are embedded as images in the PDF.
Large drawings continue in labeled, overlapping panels so their labels stay
readable. A heading stays with its first panel. PDF diagrams use a light
palette, even if your reader is in dark mode. Invalid diagrams keep their
readable source instead of stopping the export.

## Live captions

`Ctrl+Shift+L` shows subtitles for what
your computer plays, transcribed on your machine by whisper. Pick the
whisper model in **Settings → Live Captions** (`small` is a good choice on
a GPU) and download it once. Captions can be translated to English by
whisper, or to any language by your AI profile. On macOS, captions need a
loopback device such as BlackHole to hear the system audio.

```=html
<a id="meetings"></a>
```

## Meetings

Keep a conversation's audio, speaker-labelled transcript, clean text, summary,
and saved questions together. Transcription and speaker detection run on your
computer; proofreading, summaries, and questions use the active AI profile.
Choose a Built-in profile to keep that work on your device too.

### Record and revisit

Turn on **🎙 Meeting** in the captions bar, then **Start**. The captions
also record what your computer plays and your microphone (shown as
"Me"), and label the other speakers by voice as they talk. **Stop & save**
(or `Esc`, or the hotkey) saves the meeting: the whole audio is
transcribed again with the time of each word, the speakers are told
apart by voice, and your AI profile proofreads it into a clean text with
a title. A notification says when it's ready.

**Tray → Meetings** lists them. Press play and the word being said is
highlighted; click a line to play from it, double-click to edit it. Click
a speaker to rename them (every line follows); their menu merges them
into another speaker or remembers their voice, so later meetings name
them by themselves. **Copy** gives the clean text, Markdown or subtitles;
**Redo** proofreads or finds the speakers again; right-click a meeting to
rename or delete it.

### Summaries and questions

The **Summary** tab writes an overview, key points, decisions and action
items with your AI profile (a long meeting is read in parts first), and
under it you can ask your own questions, such as *What did Alex say about
me?* Answers cite the times they come from and are saved with the
meeting; **🔊 Read** reads the summary or an answer aloud.

### Recording setup

Use a headset if you can: through speakers the microphone hears the call
too. GhostPen drops those lines (same words, same moment), but a headset
keeps your track clean. The speaker model (WeSpeaker, 26 MB) downloads the
first time a meeting is saved; **Settings → Speech → Meetings** has the
options.

## Dictation

`Ctrl+Shift+D`, speak, then
`Enter`. The transcript is proofread by your AI profile (turn
**Polish** off for the raw text) and copied to the clipboard.

## Read aloud

GhostPen reads text aloud with a natural voice ([Kokoro](https://huggingface.co/hexgrad/Kokoro-82M))
that runs on your computer: nothing is sent anywhere.

- **The Read action**: select text, open the menu and pick **Read**. It
  reads the selection as it is, with no AI pass, in a voice for the
  language of the text, which GhostPen works out from the text itself.
- **🔊 Speak** on a result: when the menu shows a result, **Speak** (or the
  `S` key) reads it. A translated result is read in the target language's
  voice.
- **The summary window**: **Read summary** and **Read page**.

Press **■ Stop**, `S` again, or `Esc` to stop. Long texts are read in
sentence chunks, so the voice starts after the first sentence instead of
waiting for the whole text.

The first time, GhostPen downloads the voice model (about 135 MB) and a
voice for the language (about 0.5 MB each), checked against their SHA-256.
**Settings → Voices** lists them, downloads or deletes them, and shows
whether the voice runs on the CPU or the GPU (with a GPU build, through
Vulkan or CUDA). Voices: English (US and UK), Spanish, French, Portuguese,
Italian, Japanese, Chinese and Hindi. The pronunciation data ships with
GhostPen, so no extra package is needed.

From a terminal:

```
ghostpen --say "Good morning" --lang es --voice ef_dora
```

The binaries that read aloud include espeak-ng (GPL-3.0-or-later) for
pronunciation; GhostPen's own code stays MIT.

## Text from images

Copy an image (a screenshot) and trigger GhostPen: **Extract text** reads
it with a vision model through your profile, and the text becomes the
selection. It works with a vision model on an endpoint (e.g. `gemma4:e4b`
in Ollama) or with a built-in model that **reads images**: every catalog
model has an image projector, downloaded with it (or **Add image support**
in Settings → Built-in models for one you already have), and GhostPen runs
it on your GPU.

# Hotkeys

The three hotkeys are set in **Settings → Behaviour**. On Wayland they go
through the desktop's GlobalShortcuts portal, which asks you to confirm
them the first time. Where there is no portal, bind the command line
flags in your compositor instead; a running GhostPen receives them:

```
ghostpen --trigger      # the menu
ghostpen --voice-input  # dictation
ghostpen --captions     # captions
```

Hyprland, for example:

```
bind = CTRL SHIFT, A, exec, ghostpen --trigger
```

# Command line

`ghostpen-cli` runs an action from a terminal with the same settings:

```
echo "teh quick brown fox" | ghostpen-cli proofread
ghostpen-cli translate --lang French --stream "Good morning"
ghostpen-cli profiles
ghostpen-cli models
```

To inspect the application log without opening a window:

```
ghostpen logs
ghostpen logs --follow
```

# Where things are

| | Linux | Windows | macOS |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Settings | `~/.config/dev.ghostpen.Oriel/` | `%APPDATA%\dev.ghostpen.Oriel\` | `~/Library/Application Support/dev.ghostpen.Oriel/` |
| Models (whisper, built-in) | `~/.local/share/GhostPen/models/` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\GhostPen\models\` | `~/Library/Application Support/GhostPen/models/` |
| Voices (read aloud) | `~/.local/share/GhostPen/tts/` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\GhostPen\tts\` | `~/Library/Application Support/GhostPen/tts/` |
| Meetings | `~/Documents/GhostPen/Meetings/` (your Documents folder) | `%USERPROFILE%\Documents\GhostPen\Meetings\` | `~/Documents/GhostPen/Meetings/` |
| Known voices | `~/.local/share/GhostPen/voices.json` | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\GhostPen\voices.json` | `~/Library/Application Support/GhostPen/voices.json` |

Settings from the earlier Rust/Tauri GhostPen are imported on the first
start, and its downloaded whisper models are reused.

# Build from source

GhostPen is built with [Oriel](https://highercomve.github.io/Oriel/):

```
git clone https://github.com/highercomve/GhostPen
cd GhostPen
oriel doctor --fix   # Zig, Node.js, …
oriel build          # zig-out/bin/ghostpen and ghostpen-cli
oriel build -Dcuda   # with an NVIDIA GPU (needs the CUDA toolkit)
oriel build -Dvulkan # with any GPU through Vulkan (Vulkan headers, glslc)
oriel package        # deb, rpm, AppImage / setup.exe / .app and .dmg
```
